  ],
  "text": "Per unit of energy, nuclear has one of the lowest death rates of any source. Including renewables. That is not what most people expect, and the reason is how the deaths arrive. Coal kills continuously and invisibly, through air quality, in numbers nobody photographs. Nuclear kills rarely and visibly, in events with names everyone remembers. Count deaths per terawatt hour, including Chernobyl and Fukushima, and coal is hundreds of times worse. The energy density is extreme. One uranium pellet holds about as much as a tonne of coal. So the waste is small. All the spent fuel America has ever made would cover one field a few metres deep. Now the honest problems. It is expensive, it is slow to build, and overruns are normal. And the waste stays dangerous a very long time, which is a political problem more than a technical one. The numbers are surprising enough without anybody arguing. Look at them and decide. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
